'' THE OUTPUT NAME IS NO LONGER REQUIRED, and this file used to assert the opposite.
''
'' It was not-implemented.test, and it pinned this:
''
''     error TKO0992: not implemented in this build: compilation -- this build
''     implements the phase 2 skeleton only (source loading and diagnostics)
''
'' The message was true in phase 2 and the condition behind it was never what it
'' described: it was 'len( outputPath ) = 0'. Every phase after the second made the
'' text false and none of them changed it, so 'tiko test.bas' -- the first thing
'' anyone types -- answered that the compiler was a stub. The test kept it that way.
''
'' tiko now names the output after the first input module, as fbc does: prog.bas ->
'' prog.exe on Windows, prog with no extension on Linux, sub/deep.bas -> sub/deep.exe
'' keeping the directory, and under '-c' one object per module named after that
'' module. Measured against fbc 1.20.0 rather than assumed.
''
'' WHAT THIS FILE CAN ACTUALLY CHECK is the half that needs no toolchain. The naming
'' itself is only observable as a file appearing on disk, and the runner has no
'' directive for that -- so it is verified by hand and said so in the commit, rather
'' than asserted here in a form that would really be testing whether clang and the
'' runtime happen to be installed.
''
'' The invariant below is the one that matters most, because getting it wrong is
'' silent and expensive: A DUMP MODE MUST NOT ACQUIRE AN OUTPUT NAME. '--emit-ir'
'' and '--dump-ir' reach the same branch as a real build, and inside it the codegen
'' test is 'emitIr orelse wantsOutput'. Had the default been applied unconditionally,
'' every --emit-ir run -- and every corpus sweep built on one -- would have started
'' emitting objects and linking them.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --emit-ir tests/source/plain.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0

'' Still IR on stdout, not a build.
'' EXPECT-OUT: target triple = "x86_64-w64-windows-gnu"

'' And the message that sent people to this file is gone for good.
'' EXPECT-NOT: TKO0992
'' EXPECT-NOT: phase 2 skeleton
